# Configuration for the vsmp systemd unit.
# Install to /etc/default/vsmp  (the path named by EnvironmentFile= in the unit)
#
# Apply changes with:  sudo systemctl restart vsmp
# No daemon-reload needed -- this file is read at service start, not by systemd
# itself.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The movie file to play, relative to WorkingDirectory, or an absolute path.
#
# This is the movie itself. There is nothing to prepare: the player seeks into
# the single file for each frame and no longer needs it pre-sliced into
# sections. If an old <movie>/ directory of *_section*.mp4 files is still
# sitting on the Pi, it is dead weight and can be deleted.
#
# Dots in the name are fine now. Name handling uses pathlib (recommendation 31),
# so a release-style name like
#
#   Howl's.Moving.Castle.2004.1080p.x265-Rapta.mp4
#
# works as-is. It previously did not: the old code derived the movie directory
# with filename.split(".")[0], which read that name as a directory called
# "Howl's" holding clips named "Howl's_section0.Moving", and found nothing.
# Renaming is no longer necessary.
#
# Quote the value. systemd's EnvironmentFile parser applies shell-like quoting,
# so quotes let the name contain spaces or an apostrophe safely. The unit
# references it as ${VSMP_MOVIE}, which systemd expands to exactly one argument
# with whitespace preserved -- unlike $VSMP_MOVIE, which splits at whitespace
# into several arguments.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VSMP_MOVIE="howls_moving_castle.mp4"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional extra arguments, appended to the command line.
#
# Useful values:
#
#   --contrast 1.6     Boost contrast before the 1-bit dither. The default of
#                      1.0 is a no-op, which matches what the code has actually
#                      been doing. The right value depends on the film and has
#                      to be judged by eye on the panel.
#
#   --count-frames     Count frames exactly at startup instead of trusting the
#                      container. This decodes the entire movie, so on a Pi it
#                      is slow -- minutes to hours. Only worth it if the log
#                      warns that the container's frame count disagrees with
#                      duration x fps.
#
#   --queue-dir /home/pi/vsmp/queue
#                      The image-upload queue the player drains, one image per
#                      150s tick. SEE THE QUEUE NOTE BELOW -- this MUST be the
#                      same directory the web server writes to.
#
# Leave empty for normal operation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VSMP_ARGS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAGE QUEUE -- SHARED DIRECTORY (read this if you run the /upload web page)
#
# The web server's /upload page converts uploaded photos into 800x480 grayscale
# PNGs and drops them into a queue directory; the player drains that SAME
# directory one image per 150s tick, then returns to the movie. For this to work
# the player and the web server MUST point at ONE directory.
#
# The player's queue directory defaults to "queue" (relative to WorkingDirectory
# = the repo) and can be overridden two ways:
#
#   * set VSMP_QUEUE below, OR
#   * add  --queue-dir /home/pi/vsmp/queue  to VSMP_ARGS above.
#
# Whichever you choose, the value MUST match VSMP_QUEUE in
# /etc/default/vsmp-web (systemd/vsmp-web.env.example). If they differ, uploads
# land where the player never looks and are never displayed. Prefer an absolute
# path so both units agree regardless of their WorkingDirectory. The web unit
# also needs WRITE access to this directory (ReadWritePaths in
# systemd/vsmp-web.service); the player only reads and claims from it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VSMP_QUEUE="/home/pi/vsmp/queue"
